The one legal case — identical structure:

```go
type A struct { X int; Y string }
type B struct { X int; Y string }

a := A{1, "hi"}
b := B(a)   // works — same names, types, order, tags
```

The compiler verifies: field names identical, types identical, order identical, struct tags identical. Different names, extra fields, different order, different tags — compile error. There's no partial matching, no 'convert the common fields.'

The mechanism behind it: two structs with identical underlying types are really the same memory layout wearing two names — conversion is a reinterpretation (the value copies; nothing is remapped). It exists for the named-type-from-same-shape case — wrapping a type in a domain name without losing conversion access.

Everything else — the real struct-to-struct needs (DTO → domain model, adding/removing fields, different names) — is manual mapping:

```go
func toDomain(d OrderDTO) Order {
    return Order{ID: d.ID, Total: d.TotalCents / 100}
}
```

The mapping function is Go's answer to what other languages solve with inheritance hierarchies or serializers. It's verbose but explicit — every field's journey is visible, nothing transforms silently.
